(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-pharm-e2-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Ophthalmic Drugs — Clinical Vignettes 2","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 27-year-old starting an ophthalmic antihistamine asks when she will know if it is working. What do you tell her?","choices":["Allow 5 to 14 days before it works at all","It should work fully within an hour","Allow six weeks","It acts within minutes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — fast onset, slower to reach full efficacy. But allow two weeks to judge the full effect. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman asks how long she can keep using her redness-relief drops. What do you tell her?","choices":["As long as her eyes stay red","No more than six weeks","No more than two weeks","No more than 72 hours"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and if there is no improvement within 72 hours she should stop and be seen. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 45-year-old needs pupil dilation for fundoscopy, and you want the pupil to stay as reactive to light as possible. Which agent?","choices":["Atropine","Phenylephrine","Tropicamide","Cyclopentolate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The sympathomimetic leaves the pupil more reactive to light than an antimuscarinic does. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"A 6-year-old is prescribed ciprofloxacin drops. Two days later the parent reports a white deposit on the eye. What is this?","choices":["A corneal ulcer forming","A fungal superinfection","Dried ointment residue","An expected white precipitate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and specific enough to ciprofloxacin to be worth warning about in advance. Seen in about one in six patients on this drug. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 33-year-old asks for a prescription for the numbing drops that helped during foreign body removal. What do you do?","choices":["Decline — not for home use","Prescribe a short course for comfort","Prescribe them with a warning not to drive","Substitute a topical steroid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The eye stays numb for 10 to 20 minutes with no blink reflex, which is unsafe unsupervised. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A 19-year-old contact lens wearer has purulent conjunctivitis. Keratitis has been excluded. Which class do you reach for, and why?","choices":["A fluoroquinolone","A macrolide, because it is soothing on the inflamed eye","An aminoglycoside, because it covers gram negatives","Sulfacetamide, because it is inexpensive"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the same class is preferred for corneal ulcers for the same reason. Because lens wearers are at high risk for Pseudomonas. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 49-year-old woman starting a prostaglandin analogue in one eye should be warned about which cosmetic effects?","choices":["Drooping of the eyelid","Longer lashes and iris colour change","Yellowing of the sclera","Permanent pupil dilation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and they matter more when only one eye is treated. Conjunctival hyperaemia is common too. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 51-year-old is prescribed a topical steroid for anterior uveitis. How long may the course run?","choices":["Less than 72 hours","Less than two weeks, as a pulse","Up to three months","As long as inflammation persists"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The risks — cataract, raised pressure, infection, delayed healing, corneal ulcers — are why. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 57-year-old needs treatment for endophthalmitis with an immediate local effect. Which route achieves this by circumventing absorption entirely?","choices":["Subconjunctival injection","Oral therapy","Intravitreal injection or device","Topical drops"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with a potential sustained effect too. Its limitation is retinal toxicity. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 26-year-old finds his vision blurs badly on pilocarpine. Why is this class particularly poorly tolerated in younger patients?","choices":["It causes iris colour change in the young","Miosis blurs vision","Younger patients absorb more drug systemically","The drug raises intraocular pressure in the young"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — fixed small pupils, myopia and visual disturbance, and compliance suffers for it. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"An 18-month-old needs pressure-lowering therapy. Which class is contraindicated?","choices":["Prostaglandin analogues","Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors","Cholinergic agonists","Alpha-2 agonists"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — contraindicated under two years because of central nervous system depression and apnoea. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"A 40-year-old is shown how to use both a drop and an ointment. Why must she cover the lacrimal duct after the DROP but not the ointment?","choices":["The ointment is absorbed entirely through the cornea","Ointments have no systemic absorption at all","Drops are sterile and ointments are not","The ointment is too viscous to drain away"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. With a drop, occluding the duct limits nasolacrimal drainage and the systemic absorption that follows it. So there is nothing to block. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A toddler swallows a bottle of a parent's redness-relief drops. Why is this dangerous, given the drug acts on alpha-1 receptors in the eye?","choices":["Systemically they act on alpha-2","Systemically they act on beta-1 receptors","They are converted to epinephrine in the gut","They inhibit carbonic anhydrase systemically"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Local alpha-1 against systemic alpha-2 is exactly why an ingestion behaves differently from an instillation. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 66-year-old on latanoprost asks whether using it twice daily would lower his pressure further. What do you tell him?","choices":["Yes, but only if pressure remains high","It makes no difference either way","No — it INHIBITS the effect","Yes, twice daily is more effective"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and counterintuitive enough to be worth stating plainly. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 71-year-old on dorzolamide complains of a persistent bitter taste and stinging on instillation. What do you tell him?","choices":["Both are common with this class","This suggests an allergy and the drug must stop","This means the drop is contaminated","This indicates systemic absorption and cardiac risk"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and they are common enough that warning in advance improves adherence. Bitter taste in about a quarter and stinging in about a third. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"A 70-year-old on timolol drops develops fatigue and a heart rate of 48. What has happened?","choices":["An allergic reaction to the preservative","Rebound hyperaemia","Carbonic anhydrase inhibition","Systemic beta blockade"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Worsening heart failure, bradycardia, heart block and increased airway resistance are all possible from a drop. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A 58-year-old with asthma needs a beta blocker drop for glaucoma. Which agent, and why?","choices":["Levobunolol, because it is well tolerated","Carteolol, because it is beta-2 selective","Betaxolol — beta-1 selective","Timolol, because it is more efficacious"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The nonselective agents are more efficacious in the eye but carry the respiratory risk. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 35-year-old with predictable seasonal allergy cannot tolerate antihistamine drops and is willing to start weeks before the season. Which class suits her?","choices":["An ophthalmic antibiotic","A mast cell stabiliser","A vasoconstrictor used daily through the season","A topical glucocorticoid through the season"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Slow to work — 5 to 14 days — but that matters less when treatment starts before the season. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 63-year-old is being assessed for a corneal infiltrate. Which of her features most raises the risk that it is FUNGAL?","choices":["Long-term topical steroid use","A history of migraine","Taking a beta blocker for hypertension","Wearing reading glasses"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Immunosuppression — including topical steroids — sits alongside trauma, chronic surface disease and contact lens wear. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 36-year-old has a small peripheral corneal infection. Why can a topical antibiotic reach concentrations at the eye that an intravenous one cannot?","choices":["The cornea actively transports antibiotics","Topical drugs avoid the blood-brain barrier","It gives very high LOCAL concentrations","Topical drugs have greater systemic bioavailability"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — and the trade-off is that more involved infection still needs systemic therapy. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 44-year-old with a documented sulfonamide allergy needs treatment for bacterial conjunctivitis. Which agent must be avoided?","choices":["Sulfacetamide","Bacitracin","Erythromycin","Tobramycin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is a sulfonamide, so the allergy rules it out. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 19"}]};
